{! layout("base") !}
{! block("title") !}toke — A Language Built for AI{! end !}
{! block("content") !}

<section class="hero">
  <div class="lockup"><span class="coin"></span><span class="wordmark">toke</span></div>
  <h1>A language built for <span>AI</span>.</h1>
  <p>toke: a compiled language designed for LLM code generation, with a small grammar, one canonical form and compiler verification.</p>
  <div class="pill-row">
    <span class="pill pill-gold">14 keywords</span>
    <span class="pill pill-ticket">one canonical form</span>
    <span class="pill pill-neutral">v0.4 syntax</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cta">
    <a href="/docs" class="btn btn-primary">Get started</a>
    <a href="/docs/learn/01-why-toke" class="btn btn-outline">Why toke?</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke" class="btn btn-outline">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- The Problem                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>The Problem</h2>
</div>
<p class="section-desc">Every time an AI writes code, it generates one token at a time. Each token costs compute, time, and money. Today's programming languages were designed for humans &mdash; they're full of verbose keywords, boilerplate imports, and ceremony that LLMs must produce token by token.</p>
<p class="section-desc"><strong style="color:#f5efe2">toke</strong> is a compiled programming language designed for LLM code generation. It has 14 keywords, a 55-character set, a backtrack-free grammar with bounded lookahead, and one canonical form per construct, chosen by measurement in a 46-pattern catalogue and reproduced by <code>tkc --min</code>. That makes generated code cheap to constrain during decoding, cheap for a compiler to verify afterwards, and compact to emit. Token efficiency is one measured property of toke, always reported with its tokenizer and its baseline, not the whole claim.</p>

<div class="explainer" style="max-width:860px;margin:1rem auto;padding-left:2rem;padding-right:2rem;border-left:2px solid #f0c04a;">
  <p><strong>Where toke stands on tokens today, stated plainly.</strong> Under <em>one shared tokenizer</em> (cl100k_base on both sides), toke currently costs <strong>more</strong> tokens than equivalent Python, not fewer: <strong>1.34&times;</strong> [1.22, 1.48] over the 60 Gate-1 tasks (4,787 vs 3,565; N&nbsp;=&nbsp;60) and <strong>1.30&times;</strong> on four execution-verified sample pairs (344 vs 264; N&nbsp;=&nbsp;4). In bytes &mdash; the one lane with no tokenizer assumption &mdash; toke runs <strong>0.92&times;</strong> Python (693 vs 753, N&nbsp;=&nbsp;4). The defensible win is toke against itself: rewriting those 60 tasks into canonical v0.4 cut them from 6,347 to <strong>4,787</strong> cl100k tokens, a <strong>24.6%</strong> reduction under one tokenizer. Earlier site copy claiming &ldquo;40&ndash;75% fewer tokens&rdquo; or &ldquo;a 42% reduction vs Python&rdquo; measured the toke side with a toke-trained tokenizer and the Python side with cl100k &mdash; two different tokenizers &mdash; and is withdrawn. Every figure here is sourced from <code>docs/metrics-baseline.md</code>; the <a href="/roadmap" style="color:#f0c04a;">roadmap</a> says what is being measured next and why.</p>
</div>

<div class="explainer" style="max-width:860px;margin:1rem auto;padding-left:2rem;padding-right:2rem;">
  <p><strong>What is a token?</strong> LLMs don't read characters &mdash; they read <em>tokens</em>, which are chunks of text (roughly 3&ndash;4 characters each). The statement <code>return 0;</code> costs 4 tokens every time an LLM writes it. In toke, the same statement is <code>&lt;0;</code> &mdash; and with a purpose-built tokenizer trained on toke programs, the entire return-and-close pattern <code>&lt;0;};</code> becomes a single token.</p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- See the Difference                                                 -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>See the Difference</h2>
</div>

<div class="prompt-box">
  <span class="prompt-label">Prompt</span>
  <p>"Write a complete program that prints the 10th Fibonacci number"</p>
</div>

<p class="section-desc">Each panel shows how <em>that language's own tokenizer lane</em> segments a complete, runnable program: the toke panel under toke's v0.3 purpose-built BPE, the Python, C and Java panels under cl100k_base. <strong>The two lanes are different tokenizers, so no reduction may be read across the panels</strong> &mdash; doing so measures the toke tokenizer's training bias rather than the language, which is the error the old &ldquo;42% vs Python&rdquo; headline made. For the same-tokenizer numbers, see the box above.</p>

<div class="tv-tabs" style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem;">
  <div class="tv-tab-bar">
    <button class="tv-tab-btn active" data-tab="see-toke">toke &mdash; 24 (toke BPE v03)</button>
