<div class="comparison-note">
  <p><strong>Documentation costs scale within a lane; toke's don't.</strong> Under cl100k_base, adding docstrings and descriptive names takes this Python program from 41 to 82 tokens and this Java program from 62 to 102 &mdash; that growth is a same-tokenizer comparison and stands. The toke panel is unchanged at 24 toke-BPE tokens because toke has no comment syntax; documentation lives in companion files. The toke count is in a different tokenizer lane, so the toke figure is <em>not</em> subtracted from the others here: what this section shows is the shape of comment cost inside each language, not a cross-language reduction.</p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- Why This Matters                                                   -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
</div>

<div class="features">
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Compact in Bytes, Measured in Every Lane</h3>
    <p>Over four execution-verified toke/Python pairs, toke is <strong>0.92&times;</strong> Python in raw bytes (693 vs 753) &mdash; the lane that assumes no tokenizer. Under cl100k_base on both sides it costs <strong>1.30&times;</strong> the tokens (344 vs 264), and <strong>1.34&times;</strong> [1.22, 1.48] over the 60 Gate-1 tasks. Rewriting those 60 tasks into canonical v0.4 cut them <strong>24.6%</strong> (6,347 &rarr; 4,787 cl100k tokens), which is the same-tokenizer win the language actually delivers. The old &ldquo;42% vs Python&rdquo; figure crossed two tokenizers and is withdrawn. <a href="/tokenizer" style="color:#f0c04a;">Try the live tokenizer &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Designed for Correctness</h3>
    <p>A backtrack-free grammar with bounded lookahead (up to 3 tokens), 14 keywords, and a 55-character set. No ambiguity, no implicit coercions, full static type checking, and structured diagnostics with stable codes, machine-parseable spans and a fix field. That is what makes a constrained-decoding mask cheap and gives a repair loop something to consume. It is also demonstrably not sufficient on its own: the Gate 2 model reached 100% compile Pass@1 and <strong>55.6%</strong> functional correctness.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Standard Library with C Runtime</h3>
    <p>30+ standard library modules with C runtime implementations &mdash; strings, files, JSON, HTTP server, database, crypto, encoding, encryption, JWT auth, WebSocket, SSE, routing, templates, CSV, math, LLM integration, charting, dashboards, and machine learning. Common operations become single function calls.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Open Source</h3>
    <p>MIT and Apache 2.0 licensed. Built in public. Every component &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke">compiler</a>, <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke">spec</a>, <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke-model">corpus &amp; tokenizer</a>, <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke-eval">benchmarks</a>, <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke-web">docs</a>, <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke-mcp">MCP server</a> &mdash; is open for contribution and enterprise use.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- Quick Example                                                      -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>Quick Example</h2>
</div>

<div class="code-window" style="max-width:660px;margin:1rem auto 0.75rem;">
  <div class="titlebar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
  <pre><span class="kw">m</span>=hello;
<span class="kw">i</span>=io:std.io;

<span class="kw">f</span>=<span class="fn">main</span>():<span class="kw">i64</span>{
  io.<span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="str">"Hello, world!"</span>);
  <span class="kw">&lt;</span><span class="num">0</span>;
};</pre>
</div>
<p class="section-desc">A complete toke program. <code style="background:#2a2317;border:1px solid #2a2317;border-radius:3px;padding:0.15em 0.35em;font-size:0.82em;color:#f0c04a;">m=</code> declares the module. <code style="background:#2a2317;border:1px solid #2a2317;border-radius:3px;padding:0.15em 0.35em;font-size:0.82em;color:#f0c04a;">i=</code> imports a library. <code style="background:#2a2317;border:1px solid #2a2317;border-radius:3px;padding:0.15em 0.35em;font-size:0.82em;color:#f0c04a;">f=</code> declares a function. <code style="background:#2a2317;border:1px solid #2a2317;border-radius:3px;padding:0.15em 0.35em;font-size:0.82em;color:#f0c04a;">&lt;</code> returns a value. Every statement ends with <code style="background:#2a2317;border:1px solid #2a2317;border-radius:3px;padding:0.15em 0.35em;font-size:0.82em;color:#f0c04a;">;</code>. No boilerplate, no ceremony.</p>

